== Marital Unity and Sexual Faithfulness

=== §. 1. #emph[Of the heads of those common-mutual duties.]

#smallcaps[In] the first part of this treatise concerning #emph[man] and #emph[wife,] it has been declared who are to be counted as such. In this second part their #emph[common-mutual duties] are to be set out. These are either #emph[absolutely necessary]#footnote[De necessitate ad esse.] for the #emph[being] and #emph[abiding] of marriage, or #footnote[De honestate ad benè esse.] needful and requisite for its #emph[well-being] and #emph[well-abiding,] that is, for the good condition of marriage, and for a commendable and comforting life together.

There are two kinds of the former:

+ #emph[Marital unity.]
+ #emph[Marital chastity.]

The latter may also be brought under two heads: for they are either such as the married couple are to perform #emph[mutually,] #emph[each to the other,] or such as both of them are to perform #emph[jointly] to #emph[others.]

Those mutual duties are:

+ #emph[A loving affection for one another.]
+ #emph[A provident care for one another.]

Under that #emph[provident care] I include both the #emph[means] by which it may be better carried out (which is #emph[cohabitation]) and the #emph[matter] in which it consists:

And this concerns the #emph[soul], the #emph[body], the #emph[good name], and the #emph[property] of each other.

The #emph[joint] duties which are to be performed #emph[to others] concern those who are #emph[in the house] and those who are #emph[outside the house.]

Those who are in the house are:

+ #emph[Members] of the family.
+ #emph[Guests] who come to the family.

Many more particulars are included under these general heads, which I intend to deliver one by one as I come to them, each in its own proper place.

=== §. 2. #emph[Of marital unity.]

The first, highest, chief and most absolutely necessary common-mutual duty between man and wife is #emph[marital unity,] by which husband and wife count one another to be #emph[one flesh,] and accordingly preserve the inviolable union by which they are knit together. This is the duty which the apostle commands husbands and wives in these words: “A wife #footnote[1 Cor 7:10, 11.] must not separate from her husband,” and, “A husband must not divorce his wife.” He speaks there of renouncing each other, and of making the marriage bond void and of no effect. He would have that bond kept firm and inviolable, and would have the #emph[two] who are made #emph[one] by it constantly remain #emph[one,] and not make themselves #emph[two] again. This #emph[marital unity] is so necessary that it must not be broken up or dissolved, even though one is a Christian and the other a pagan. “If a brother #footnote[1 Cor 7:12, 13.] has an unbelieving wife,” says the apostle, “he must not divorce her. And if a woman has an unbelieving husband, she must not divorce him.”

There are two chief reasons for this inviolable union: one taken from the #emph[author] of marriage, the other from its #emph[nature.]

1\. The #emph[author] of marriage is God.#footnote[Gen 2:18, 22, 24] It is his ordinance, and it is he who by his ordinance has made #emph[two] into #emph[one flesh.] Now note the consequence which Christ draws from this, as a settled case and an undeniable principle: “What God has joined together, #footnote[Matt 19:6.] let no man separate.” If #emph[no man,] then neither wife nor husband himself.

2\. Such is the #emph[nature] of the marriage bond that it makes #emph[two] into #emph[one,] and #footnote[See Treatise 1, §. 79, 80.] binds those two together more firmly than any other bond can bind any other two together. How then should they be #emph[two] again?

=== §. 3. #emph[Of desertion.]

The vice contrary to marital unity is #emph[desertion.] It is when one of the married couple, out of indignation against the true religion and utter detestation of it, or some other such cause, openly renounces all marital unity, withdraws himself or herself from all society with the other, and lives among infidels, idolaters, heretics or other such persecutors, among whom a faithful Christian cannot live with safety of life or a good conscience. And though every good means that can be thought of is used to win back the party who has departed, nothing will prevail, but that party obstinately persists in renouncing all marital fellowship.

This #emph[desertion] is so grave in the case of marriage that it #footnote[#emph[Infidelis discedens in matrimonium peccare dignoscitur: itaque non est ei fides seruanda coniugij. Ambr. in] 1 Cor 7. \[An unbeliever who departs is seen to sin against the marriage; so the faith of marriage need not be kept with him.\]] frees the innocent party from any further seeking after the other. In this respect the apostle says, “If the unbeliever leaves, let him go. A brother or sister is not under bondage in such cases,” 1 Cor 7:15. By bondage he means the subjection of marriage (because of which neither of the married persons has power over his own body, but each over the other’s). Now those who are not under this bondage are not bound to seek after it. Therefore that #emph[desertion] on the offender’s #footnote[τὴν λύσιν ἐκεῖνος ποιεῖ. #emph[Phot. in] 1 Cor 7. #emph[Luth. de matrim. Calu. epist.] 227. #emph[Beza in lib. de Diuort.]] part is such a dissolution of marriage as frees the innocent party from its bondage. In many reformed churches overseas #emph[desertion] is held to dissolve the very bond of marriage so far that liberty is given to the forsaken party to marry another. And it is also applied to other cases than the one mentioned above: as when an #footnote[#emph[P. Martyr in] 1 Cor 7:13. #emph[Melanct. in loc. de Coniug.]] infidel, idolater or heretic departs from one of the true religion for other causes than hatred of religion; or when both man and wife have lived as idolaters among idolaters, #footnote[#emph[Zanchius de Oper. Dei lib.] 4. #emph[cap.] 1.] and one of them, being converted to the true faith, leaves his home among idolaters and goes to those who profess the true faith, but can by no means get the other party to move; or when one of the true religion departs from another of the same profession of faith, and will by no means be brought to live with the party left behind, but openly shows a stubborn obstinacy. In such cases, the matter being heard and judged by the magistrate, the marriage bond may be broken, and liberty given to the forsaken party to marry another. But because our church has no such custom, and our law has not settled such cases, I leave them to the custom of other churches.

=== §. 4. #emph[Of marital chastity.]

The second necessary common-mutual marriage duty is #emph[marital chastity. Chastity] in a broad sense is taken for every kind of purity in soul or body: in this sense the apostle calls the church of God “a chaste virgin.”#footnote[2 Cor 11:2.] But in the sense in which we use it here, it belongs especially to the body. It is that virtue by which “we possess our vessels”#footnote[1 Thess 4:4.] (to use the apostle’s phrase) “in holiness and honor”; or, more plainly to our purpose, by which we keep our bodies undefiled.

Chastity thus restricted to the body is that of

- #emph[Single life.]
- #emph[Wedlock.]

That of single life is opposed to fornication. It is either the chastity of those who were never married: such a one was Saint Paul, and in this respect he wishes that all were as he was, 1 Cor 7:7. Or it is the chastity of those who are lawfully freed from the bond of marriage: such a one the apostle calls #footnote[ὄντως χήρα. 1 Tim 5:5. #emph[Paphnutius congressum cum legitima vxore castitatem esse dicebat.] \[Paphnutius said that union with one’s lawful wife is chastity.\] #emph[Socr. hist. Eccl. l.] 1. #emph[cap.] 11.] “a true widow.” The chastity of wedlock is that virtue by which married parties, observing the lawful and honorable use of marriage, keep their bodies from being defiled with another’s flesh. So the apostle commands #emph[wives] to be “chaste,” Titus 2:5. So #footnote[Qui leges coniugij seruat homini continenti par sit. Greg. Naz. Cygn. Carm.] those who keep the laws of wedlock are as chaste as those who abstain altogether.

Here, by the way, note the folly of our adversaries, who think there is no chastity except that of single persons. So in their speeches and writings #footnote[#emph[Clerici castitatem professi matrimonium contrahere non possunt.] \[Clerics who have professed chastity cannot contract marriage.\] #emph[Concil. Trid.] §. 24. #emph[Can.] 9.] they set chastity and marriage against one another as two contraries.

Some of their holy fathers and popes, and those not the least learned nor of the worst repute among them, have inferred in their arguments against priests’ marriage that #emph[marriage is a living in the flesh, a sowing to the flesh, a pollution of the flesh.]#footnote[#emph[Siricius & Innocentius] 3. #emph[Gratian. Decr. distinct.] 31. 82. 1 Cor 7:5.] To that purpose they urge Saint Paul’s advice to man and wife to abstain so that they may give themselves to fasting and prayer: but this is directly contrary to the apostle’s intent. For,

1\. He speaks there of extraordinary humbling. 2. He adds this limitation, “for a time.” 3. He does not say simply, “that you#super[+] may pray,” but “so you#super[+] may devote yourselves#super[+]” (or have #footnote[σχολάζητε. #emph[Non dixit simpliciter vt oretis, sed vt vacetis orationi: ac si rem impediat, non coinquinet. Chrys. in] 1 Cor 7. #emph[hom.] 19.] leisure) “to prayer”: as if it only hindered prayer, but did not pollute it.

But how can the spots and blots on marriage named above agree with the beauty and glory with which the apostle sets it out in these words, “marriage is honorable in all,” Heb 13:4? If marriage were what the papists make it out to be, the #emph[marriage bed] would be very unfittingly called “undefiled.”

See how contrary the spirits of Saint Paul and of their popes were! Far more contrary, I know well, than chastity and marriage are.

But to return to our matter: it is clear that married persons may be chaste, and accordingly they ought to be chaste. To this end the apostle, counseling men and women to have wives and husbands in order to avoid fornication, inserts this word OWN (“each man should have his OWN wife, #footnote[1 Cor 7:2 ἑαυτοῦ. ἴδιον.] and each woman her OWN husband”), by which he implies that they should have relations with no one else. What #footnote[Prov 5:19. See more on this text in Treatise 4, §. 11.] Solomon says of a husband must, by the rule of relation, be applied to a wife. As the man must be satisfied at all times with his wife, and even enraptured with her love, so the woman must be satisfied at all times with her husband, and even enraptured with his love. By the same rule husbands must take the command given to wives, “to be chaste,”#footnote[Titus 2:5.] as directed to themselves also, and be #emph[chaste.] Isaac and Rebekah faithfully and mutually performed this duty to each other.

1\. It was one main end for which marriage was ordained (especially since the #footnote[Reasons for marital chastity.] fall of man): to live #emph[chastely.] The apostle implies this where he says, “to avoid fornication,” let every man have his own wife, and let every woman have her own husband. #footnote[1\. Chastity is one end of marriage. 1 Cor 7:2. #emph[Ibid. v.] 9.] And again, “If they cannot control themselves, let them marry.”

2\. By chastity a godly seed is preserved on earth. With this reason the prophet Malachi urges this duty. For after he had said that the Lord “made them one,” meaning that he made two into one flesh #footnote[2\. By chastity a godly seed is preserved. Mal 2:16.] by marriage, he draws this exhortation from it: “So guard yourselves#super[+] in your#super[+] spirit, and let no one break faith with the wife of his youth.”

3\. A special part of the honor of marriage consists in #footnote[3\. Chastity is the honor of marriage. Heb 13:4.] chastity. So the apostle, having given marriage this high commendation, that it is “honorable in all,” adds this clause (“and the bed undefiled”) to show the reason for that honor. It is as if he had said, Because the marriage bed is in itself a bed undefiled, marriage is therefore in itself honorable, and it remains honorable just so far as the bed remains undefiled.

=== §. 5. #emph[Of adultery.]

The vice contrary to marital chastity is #emph[adultery,] one of the most grievous vices in that state: a vice by which the way is opened to #emph[divorce.] This is clear and evident from the ruling of Christ himself on that point, first #footnote[Matt 5:32.] set out in his Sermon on the Mount, and again #footnote[Matt 19:9. #emph[Christus ab adultera viro separari permittit.] \[Christ permits a husband to be separated from an adulterous wife.\] #emph[Greg. Naz. Orat.] 36. #emph[Vir post fornicationem non est vir,] \[After fornication the husband is no longer a husband,\] #emph[Chrys. hom.] 19. #emph[in] 1 Cor 7.] repeated in his conference with the Pharisees, where, condemning unjust divorces, he makes an exception of divorce for adultery.

And there is great reason for it. For #footnote[1 Cor 6:16.] the adulterer makes himself one flesh with his harlot. Why then should he remain one flesh with his wife? “The two,” says the law, “will be one flesh”: not three. The same may be said of a wife who commits adultery.

=== §. 6. #emph[Of pardoning adultery on repentance.]

#emph[Question.] Since adultery gives just cause for divorce, may this fault, on the repentance of the guilty person, be so forgiven that the innocent person seeks no divorce, but both continue to live together in wedlock as before?

#emph[Answer.] Though it is not fitting in this case to impose it on the innocent party as an inviolable law to keep the guilty party because of repentance (for we have no direct and strict warrant for it), yet I do not doubt that they may do so if they will; and, where there is no just objection to the contrary, they ought to do so. For the law of divorce did not require anyone to sue for the bill of divorce, but only gave them liberty to use that punishment if they saw cause. I do not doubt that, as warrant for this liberty, we may take God’s pattern in keeping churches and people after they have committed spiritual adultery, and Christ’s forgiving the woman who had committed adultery. For “since Christ said to an adulteress, ‘Neither do I condemn you; go and sin no more,’ who cannot see that a husband ought to forgive what he sees the Lord of both husband and wife has forgiven, and that he ought not to call her an adulteress whose fault he believes has been blotted out by the mercy of God on her repentance?”#footnote[#emph[Postquam Christus ait adulterae, nec ego te damnabo: vade, deinceps noli peccare, quis non intelligat, debere maritum ignoscere quod videt ignouisse Dominum amborum: nec iam se debere adulteram dicere cuius poenitentis crimen diuina credit miseratione deletam? Aug. de adult. Coniug. lib.] 2. #emph[cap.] 6.]

=== §. 7. #emph[Of the difference between adultery in a man and in a wife.]

#emph[Question.] Is the bond of marriage violated as much on the #footnote[Adultery is as great a sin in a man as in his wife. #emph[Lex Jul. de Adult. l.] 1. #emph[Orif. Codic. in l. Illust. Greg. Sayr. Clau. reg. l.] 9. #emph[c.] 16.] man’s part when he commits adultery as on the woman’s when she does?

#emph[Answer.] Though the ancient Romans and the canonists have made the woman’s fault in this kind far heavier than the man’s, and given the man more privileges than the woman, yet I do not see how that difference in the sin can stand with the tenor of God’s word. I do not deny that more troubles may follow from the woman’s fault than from the man’s: such as greater disgrace before men, worse disturbance #footnote[More troubles come from a woman’s adultery than from a man’s.] of the family, more mistaking of legitimate and illegitimate children, and the like. The man cannot know which are his own children as well as the woman can. He may take illegitimate children to be his own, and so settle the inheritance on them; and he may suspect his own to be illegitimate, and so deprive them of their patrimony. But the woman is free from all such mistakes. Yet with respect to the breaking of wedlock and the transgression against God, the sin of either party is the same. God’s word makes no difference between them. At the beginning God said of them both, “the two will be one flesh”:#footnote[Matt 19:5.] not only is the woman made #emph[one flesh] with the man, but the man also with the woman. #footnote[1 Cor 7:3, 4.] Their power over one another in this respect is also the same. If on just occasion they abstain, #footnote[#emph[v.] 5.] it must be by mutual consent. If the husband leaves his wife, #footnote[#emph[v.] 15.] she is as free as he would be if she left him. Accordingly the punishment #footnote[#emph[Tanto grauius viros puniri oportet, quanto magis ad eos pertinet & virtute vincere, & exemplo regere foeminas. Aug. de adult. Coniug. l.] 2. #emph[c.] 8.] which by God’s law was to be inflicted on adulterers is the same, whether the man or the woman is the offender (Deut 22:22). If any difference is made, it is fitting that adulterous husbands be punished all the more severely, since it belongs to them all the more to excel in virtue and to govern their wives by example.

=== §. 8. #emph[Of the heinousness of adultery.]

But to return to showing how heinous adultery is: I find no sin in the whole of Scripture set out so conspicuously in all its separate colors as this one. For besides being #footnote[7\. Commandment.] forbidden by name in the Ten Commandments, it is further expressly branded as committed:

1\. Against #emph[each person of the holy Trinity:] the #emph[Father] (whose #footnote[Prov 2:17.] covenant is broken), the #emph[Son] (whose #footnote[1 Cor 6:16.] members are made the members of a harlot), and the #emph[Holy Spirit] (whose #footnote[1 Cor 6:19.] temple is polluted).

2\. Against one’s #emph[neighbor:] the #footnote[Gen 34:31.] party with whom the sin is committed (for this sin cannot be committed by one person alone); the #emph[husband] and #emph[wife] of each party#footnote[Prov 6:35.] (who cannot rest content with any satisfaction); the #emph[children]#footnote[Gen 21:10. Judg 11:2. Deut 23:2.] born in adultery (whom the adulterers brand with an indelible mark of disgrace, and deprive of many privileges they might otherwise enjoy); the #emph[relatives by marriage]#footnote[Lev 21:9. Gen 34:7. 2 Sam 13:22.] and #emph[friends] of each party (to whom the grief and disgrace of this foul sin reaches); the #emph[whole family] belonging to either of them (for #footnote[Job 31:12. 2 Sam 12:10.] this is like a fire in a house); the #emph[town, city] and #emph[nation] where such unclean birds roost (for #footnote[Gen 34:27. Judg 20:10. Hos 4:3.] all of them lie open to the vengeance of God for this sin); and the very #emph[church of God] (whose #footnote[Mal 2:15.] holy seed is hindered by this sin).

3\. Against the #emph[parties] themselves who commit this sin, and that against their #footnote[Prov 6:32.] souls, #footnote[1 Cor 6:18.] bodies, #footnote[Prov 6:33.] name, #footnote[Prov 6:26.] property, and all that belongs to them.

As this sin is in itself a sinful sin, so by the bitter and #footnote[The bitter fruits of adultery.] cursed fruits that come from it, it is made sinful beyond measure. For

1\. By it the affection of husbands and wives (which of all others ought to be the most inviolable) is so alienated that it is seldom reconciled again.

2\. By it the property of the family is much wasted: the adulterous husband spends on his harlot what he should provide for his family with, and the adulterous wife steals what she can from her husband.

3\. By it husbands and wives are stirred up to wish for, and long for, one another’s death: and not only to wish it inwardly in their hearts, but also to carry it out outwardly in deed.

4\. If a galling and terrifying conscience does not arise from this sin (as it often does), then something worse does: a seared conscience, a hard heart, a reprobate mind, and a shameless face.

So God deals with such sinners accordingly. In #footnote[God’s judgments against adultery.] his soul #footnote[Prov 22:14.] he hates them. By his word he has denounced many fearful judgments against them, both in #footnote[Hos 4:2, 3.] this world and in #footnote[Rev 21:8.] the world to come (against no sin more). This sin is reckoned one of the chief causes of the greatest judgments ever inflicted in the world: of the #footnote[Gen 6:2, #emph[etc.]] universal flood; of the #footnote[Ezek 16:50.] fire and brimstone which destroyed Sodom and Gomorrah; of #footnote[Lev 18:28.] Canaan’s spewing out its inhabitants; of the #footnote[Num 25:9.] plague which destroyed 24,000 in one day; and of the #footnote[Jer 5:7, 8, 9.] Israelites’ captivity, and the like. By all these we see that fearful sentence proved true: “God will judge the sexually immoral and adulterers.”#footnote[Heb 13:4.] Now consider this: #footnote[Heb 10:31.] “It is a fearful thing to fall into the hands of the living God.”

=== §. 9. #emph[Of remedies against adultery, and in particular of] due benevolence, #emph[and of defect or excess in it.]

For preventing this heinous sin, God’s word has prescribed many other remedies, which I pass over: such as #footnote[Prov 6:25.] a diligent keeping of the #emph[heart] (so that lustful thoughts do not come from it); of the #emph[eyes]#footnote[Job 31:1.] (so that they do not wander to the beauty or handsomeness of anyone’s person, or to lascivious pictures, or any other such allurements); of the #emph[ears]#footnote[Prov 7:21.] (so that they do not listen to any enticements of others); of the #emph[tongue]#footnote[Eph 5:4.] (so that it utters no unchaste and corrupt talk); of the #emph[lips]#footnote[Prov 7:13.] (so that they take no delight in wanton kisses); of the #emph[hands]#footnote[Prov 6:29.] (so that they use no wanton caresses); of the #emph[feet]#footnote[Prov 7:25.] (so that they do not carry you too near the place where adultery may be committed); of your #emph[company]#footnote[Eph 5:7.] (so that you are not defiled with others’ wantonness and impurity); of your #emph[diet]#footnote[Gen 19:33.] (so that it is not immoderate); of your #emph[clothing]#footnote[Ezek 23:6, 15, 40.] (so that it is not gaudy and lascivious); of your #emph[time]#footnote[Gen 39:9. Prov 2:16.] (so that it is not spent in vanity and idleness). These and other such remedies, I say, I pass over. One of the best remedies that can be prescribed to married persons (next to a #footnote[Gen 39:9. Prov 2:16.] reverent fear of God, and a continual setting of him before them wherever they are) is that husband and wife delight in each other mutually, and maintain a pure and fervent love between themselves, yielding to one another that #emph[due benevolence]#footnote[1 Cor 7:2, 3, 5, 9.] (the marriage duty each owes the other) which is warranted and sanctified by God’s word, and ordained by God for this particular end. This #emph[due benevolence] (as the apostle calls it) is one of the most distinctive and essential acts of marriage, and necessary for its main and principal ends: for the preservation of chastity in those who do not have the gift of self-control, for increasing the world with legitimate offspring, and for linking the affections of the married couple more firmly together. These ends of marriage, at least the first two, are made void unless this duty is performed.

As it is called #footnote[εὔνοια.] #emph[benevolence] because it must be performed with good will and delight, willingly, readily and cheerfully, so it is said to be #footnote[ὀφειλομένη.] #emph[due] because it is a debt which the wife owes to her husband, and he to her. “The wife does not have power over her own body, but the husband. Likewise the husband does not have power over his own body, but the wife.”#footnote[1 Cor 7:4.]

I have my warrant from the apostle to prescribe this duty as a remedy against adultery. For “to avoid fornication,”#footnote[1 Cor 7:2, 3.] he advises man and wife to “render due benevolence” to one another. If, then, this question is raised #emph[(How will marriage keep men and women from adultery?),] this answer may be given from the apostle’s words: #emph[(by rendering due benevolence).] He presses this home further by declaring the injury that may follow from neglecting this duty, namely #emph[their casting themselves into the snares of Satan.]#footnote[#emph[v.] 5.] Well might he press this duty for that end, because no other means has the same force: not fasting, not keeping vigil, not hard lodging, not long travel, not much labor, not cold, not solitude, nor anything else. #footnote[Dum solitudinis me deserta vallarent, ardorem naturae ferre non poteram: quem cùm crebris ieiunijs frangerem, mens cogitationibus aestuabat. \[While the deserts of solitude walled me in, I could not bear the heat of nature; and when I broke it with frequent fasts, my mind still burned with thoughts.\] Hier. ad Rust. Mon. Lege eundem ad Eustoc. de custod. virgin.] Some who have labored hard by these means to beat down their bodies and subdue lust (but neglected the remedy named above) have nevertheless felt lust boiling in them.

There are two extremes contrary to this duty: one in the #emph[defect,] the other in the #emph[excess.]

#emph[Defect] in it is when, in case of need, it is not asked for; or, being asked for by the one, it is not yielded by the other. Some make modesty their pretext for not asking for it. But in a duty so warrantable and needful, a pretense of modesty is (to say the least) a sign of great weakness, and a cause of much iniquity. To deny this duty when it is rightly asked for is to deny a due debt, and to give Satan great advantage. The punishment inflicted on #emph[Onan] (Gen 38:9, 10) shows how great a wrong this is. From that punishment #footnote[#emph[Hebraei inquiunt perinde vt homicidam reum esse qui temerè semen profundit. Merc. in] Gen 38:9.] the Hebrews gather that this sin is a kind of murder. It is all the more heinous when hatred, stubbornness, squeamishness, fear of having too many children, or any other such motive is the cause of it.

#emph[Excess] is either in the #emph[measure] or in the #emph[time. In the measure,] when husband or wife is insatiable, provoking lust rather than assuaging it, and weakening their natural vigor rather than suppressing their unnatural appetite. Many husbands and wives are much oppressed by their bedfellows’ insatiable demands in this matter.

#emph[In the time,] when it is against #emph[piety, mercy] or #emph[modesty.]

1\. #emph[Against piety,] when no day or duty of religion, not even extraordinary days and duties of humbling, will make them forbear. #footnote[Joel 2:16.] The prophet’s bidding the bridegroom and bride come out of their chamber on the day of a fast, and #footnote[1 Cor 7:5.] the apostle’s making an exception for prayer and fasting where he commands this duty of due benevolence, show that in the time of a fast it must be forborne.

2\. #emph[Against mercy,] when one of the married couple is weak through sickness, pain, toil, travel, or any other such cause, and through that weakness is not well able to perform his duty, and yet the other will have it performed all the same. “I desire mercy, not sacrifice,” says the Lord. Shall God’s sacrifice give way to mercy, and shall not man’s or woman’s lust? For so I may well call this unseasonable desire.

#emph[Question.] What if a husband or wife remains sick, or otherwise weak, for so long that the other cannot exercise self-control?

#emph[Answer.] In such cases of necessity the body must be beaten down, and earnest prayer made for the gift of self-control. For assuredly the Lord who has brought you to that necessity will give you sufficient grace.

3\. #emph[Against modesty,] #footnote[#emph[Quo tempore si vir coierit cum muliere, dicuntur concepti foetus vitium seminis trahere: ita vt leprosi & elephantiaci ex hac conceptione nascantur. Hier. in Lam.] 1. \[If a man comes together with a woman at that time, the children conceived are said to take on the defect of the seed, so that lepers and those with elephantiasis are born of this conception.\]] when husbands require this duty at the time which under the law was called #emph[the time of a wife’s separation for her sickness] (Lev 15:19, etc.). For what can be expected from such polluted intercourse but a leprous and loathsome offspring? This kind of intemperance is expressly forbidden (Lev 18:19), and a capital punishment was inflicted on those who offended in it (Lev 20:18). Abstinence at this time is set in the list of those marks which show a man to be righteous (Ezek 18:6), and the contrary intemperance is put in the roll of the abominations which provoked God to spew out the Canaanites (Lev 18:28) and to forsake his own inheritance (Ezek 22:10).

To this kind of intemperance #footnote[#emph[Aug. de bon. coniug. ca.] 6. #emph[Amb. in] 1 Cor 7.] some refer a man’s knowing his wife after she has conceived. But I find no such thing condemned in God’s word, and I dare not make that a sin which is not condemned there. #footnote[#emph[Nemo Essenorum cum praegnante concumbit. Joseph. de bel. Jud. li.] 2. #emph[c.] 7. \[None of the Essenes lies with a pregnant woman.\]] Certain sects among the Jews are branded for this error.

1\. #emph[Objection.] No other animal will do so, so it may seem to be against nature.

#emph[Answer.] 1. I deny the argument: though some forbear, not all do.

2\. I deny the consequence: for other animals are not bound one male to one female as a husband is to his own wife. #footnote[#emph[Quod in bestijs ipso naturae iure praescriptum est, vt praegnantes ad partum vsque non coeant, hoc in hominibus sciant arbitrio relictum. Hier. in Eph.] 5. #emph[Sanchez de Matr. lib.] 9. #emph[disp.] 21, 22. #emph[agit de petendo & reddendo debito quando menstruum est: & tempore praegnationis, & dum infans lactatur.] \[He treats of asking for and rendering the debt during menstruation, in the time of pregnancy, and while the infant is nursing.\]] Besides, what beasts are bound to by nature must be left to man’s discretion.

2\. #emph[Objection.] Once a woman has conceived, no more conceptions can be expected until she is delivered.

#emph[Answer.] Conception is not the only end of this duty: for it is to be rendered to those who are barren.

#emph[Question.] What if the wife is nursing her child? Ought not her husband then to forbear?

#emph[Answer.] Because nursing is a mother’s duty, a man ought to do what he can to restrain himself.
